    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Ferdowsi put into verse the Persian prose Shahnameh and, through it, the Sasanian Khwadāy-Nāmag, carrying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>'s story from the first king, Keyumars, to the Arab conquest in the seventh century.</li><li>Some experts credit it with keeping Modern Persian close to the language of Ferdowsi's time; Mohammed Moinfar counts about 700 words of Arabic origin in it, around 9% of its vocabulary.</li><li>Rulers from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a> took names from it, inscribed its verses on city walls and commissioned illustrated copies, among the most sumptuous works of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-miniature">Persian miniature</a> painting.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Seljuk sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toghrul-iii">Toghrul III</a> is said to have recited the Shahnameh while swinging his mace in battle.</p></aside>
    </section>

