    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">977</span><span class="rf-text">Ferdowsi begins the Shahnameh, recasting in verse the prose accounts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>'s ancient history</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1010</span><span class="rf-text">Ferdowsi completes the poem after 33 years of work</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1217</span><span class="rf-text">A copy is made that, rediscovered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> in 1977, is one of the earliest known <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> of the poem</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1220</span><span class="rf-text">Al-Fath bin Ali al-Bundari makes the only known Arabic translation, for the Ayyubid ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-muazzam-isa">Al-Mu'azzam Isa</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1221</span><span class="rf-text">The Seljuk sultan of Rum Ala' al-Din Kay-kubad decorates the walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sivas">Sivas</a> with verses from the poem</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1430</span><span class="rf-text">The illuminated Baysonghori Shahnameh is made for Prince Baysunghur; it survives intact at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golestan-palace">Golestan Palace</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1829</span><span class="rf-text">Turner Macan publishes the first complete edition, based on a comparison of 17 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2008</span><span class="rf-text">The last volume of Djalal Khaleghi-Motlagh's critical edition, built on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a>, is published</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khosrow-ii.jpg" alt="Khosrow II" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-ii">Khosrow II</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:KhosrauIIGoldCoinCroppedHistoryofIran.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Classical Numismatic Group; [1], CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Shahnameh rests on an older tradition of royal history. In late Sasanian times a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-persian">Pahlavi</a> (Middle Persian) compilation, the Khwadāy-Nāmag or “Book of Kings”, told the history of the kings and heroes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> from mythical times down to the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-ii">Khosrow II</a> (590–628). It held historical information on the later Sasanian period but does not appear to have drawn on historical sources for the earlier one. For the most part it passed into a Persian prose work of the same name, compiled in Ferdowsi's earlier life in his native Tus; many such prose accounts existed, among them the Shahnameh of Abu-Mansur.</p><p>Other Pahlavi texts fed the poem, chief among them the Kar-Namag i Ardashir i Pabagan, a late Sasanian account of how <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardashir-i">Ardashir I</a> came to power, with which, according to Zabihollah Safa, various phrases and words of the poem can be matched. Some claim that Ferdowsi also used Zoroastrian nasks such as the now-lost Chihrdad. The orientalists Josef Markwart and Theodor Nöldeke held that much of the poem's world reshaped Parthian history, itself already altered in Sasanian retellings. Legends of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostam">Rostam</a> were known across the Iranian lands by the end of the Sasanian period; Markwart traced him to Parthian origins, and his home, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistan">Sakastan</a>, was the ancestral seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-suren">House of Suren</a>, one of the seven great Parthian families.</p><p>The first to put the Pahlavi chronicle into verse was Daqiqi, a poet at the Samanid court, who came to a violent end after completing only 1,000 verses.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="content">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What It Is</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/rostam"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rostam.jpg" alt="Rostam" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/rostam">Rostam</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rostam_and_the_White_Div_from_Baysanghar_Shahnameh.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Artists of the court of Bāysunghur in Herat., Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After an opening in praise of God and Wisdom, the poem recounts the creation of the world and of man as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a> believed it, then the reigns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>'s kings in three ages. The mythical age, some 2100 verses or four percent of the whole, runs from Keyumars, the first man and first king, and his grandson Hushang, who discovered fire and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadeh">Sadeh</a> Feast, through Tahmuras, Jamshid, Zahhak, Kaveh, and Fereydun and his three sons Salm, Tur and Iraj, to Manuchehr.</p><p>Almost two-thirds of the work belongs to the heroic age, from Manuchehr to the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>: the kingdom of the Kayanians, in which the heroes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistan">Sistan</a> are the backbone of the empire, Sam, his son Zal, Zal's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostam">Rostam</a> and then Faramarz. Its stories include the romance of Zal and Rudaba, the Seven Stages (or Labours) of Rostam, Rostam and Sohrab, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siyavash">Siyavash</a> and Sudaba, Bijan and Manijeh, the wars with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrasiab">Afrasiab</a>, and Rostam and Esfandyar. Three sections on Alexander, over 2,500 verses, mark the turn from mythic to historical rulers. The historical age mentions the Arsacids briefly, relates Sasanian history with a good deal of accuracy and narrates the fall of the Sasanians and the Arab conquest romantically.</p><p>Jalal Khaleghi Mutlaq finds in it moral virtues such as worship of one God, patriotism, love of family and help for the poor; other scholars read in it a contrast of good and evil drawn from Zoroastrian dualism, and Dariush Zolfaghari a war for the survival of Iranian cultural identity.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="making">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Was Made</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-of-ghazni"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mahmud-of-ghazni.jpg" alt="Mahmud of Ghazni" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-of-ghazni">Mahmud of Ghazni</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mahmud_of_Ghazni,_Ghaznavid_ruler,_conquering_Qasdar_(modern_Khuzdar)_in_India,_miniature_from_the_Jami%CA%BF_al-Tawarikh_of_Rashid_al-Din_Il-Khanid_Tabriz_Ms_Or_20_f.108v.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1314-15, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ferdowsi began the Shahnameh in 977 and completed it in March 1010, a labour of 33 years. He wrote in Early New Persian, recasting in verse what he, his contemporaries and his predecessors regarded as the account of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>'s ancient history; only a small portion, in passages scattered through the poem, is entirely his own conception. Daqiqi's 1,000 verses on the rise of the prophet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroaster">Zoroaster</a> were taken into the poem, with acknowledgment. Its style shows characteristics of both written and oral literature.</p><p>The poem has 62 stories, 990 chapters and some 50,000 rhyming couplets, more than three times the length of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a>: the longest epic poem created by a single author. Ferdowsi himself put the final edition at some sixty thousand distichs, but that is a round figure, and most of the relatively reliable <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> preserve a little over fifty thousand. According to Nizami Aruzi, the final edition sent to the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-of-ghazni">Mahmud of Ghazni</a> was prepared in seven volumes.</p><p>Ferdowsi is held to have purposefully avoided Arabic vocabulary, a view the 19th-century British Iranologist E. G. Browne also put forward. Recent study by Mohammed Moinfar counts approximately 700 words of Arabic origin, around 9% of the vocabulary; the overwhelming majority of the lexicon remains Persian in origin.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="reception">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Reception</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tahmasp-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tahmasp-i.jpg" alt="Tahmasp I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tahmasp-i">Tahmasp I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shah_Tahmasp_I_(1514-1576)_in_a_landscape_(painted_circa_1575).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Artist circa 1575, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The poem's impact on Persian historiography was immediate. Historians decorated their books with its verses, from the unknown author of the Tarikh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistan">Sistan</a>, written around 1053, to Khwand Mir, who gave Ferdowsi an extensive biography around 1523; the Arab historian Ibn Athir wrote that it might be called “the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> of 'Ajam”. Masud Sa'ad Salman recited its poems at the Ghaznavid court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> only 80 years after its composition, and poets from Asadi Tusi, whose Garshaspnama it inspired, to Nizami Ganjavi drew on its stories.</p><p>Rulers adopted it. The sultans of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> took titles from ancient Persian mythology such as Kai Khosrow and Kay Kāvus, and according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-bibi">Ibn Bibi</a>, in 1221 Ala' al-Din Kay-kubad decorated the walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sivas">Sivas</a> with its verses. The Qarakhanid dynasty called itself the “family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrasiab">Afrasiyab</a>”, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shirvanshah">Shirvanshahs</a> took many of their names from it, and Shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-i">Ismail I</a>, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavid dynasty</a>, named all his sons after its characters and used it as a reassertion of Persian royal authority. Mongol, Timurid, Turkman and Safavid patrons commissioned illustrated copies; the Shahnameh of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tahmasp-i">Shah Tahmasp</a> held a series of 250 miniatures.</p><p>Not every regime welcomed it. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-reza-pahlavi">Mohammad Reza Shah</a> its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theme-system">themes</a> of regicide and the incompetence of kings sat badly with the monarchy, and Ali Shariati opposed it for verses critical of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="survival">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Survival &amp; Rediscovery</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/persian-miniature"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/persian-miniature.jpg" alt="Persian miniature" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/persian-miniature">Persian miniature</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yusef_Zuleykha.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kamāl ud-Dīn Behzād, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Among the earliest known copies is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> of 1217, which predates the Mongol invasion and the destruction of libraries that followed it; it was rediscovered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> in 1977. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> critical edition relied heavily on a 1276 manuscript in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a> and a 1333 Leningrad manuscript, the latter now considered secondary.</p><p>Illustrated copies are among the most sumptuous examples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-miniature">Persian miniature</a> painting. The “Great Mongol” or Demotte Shahnameh was produced under the Ilkhanid sultan Abu Sa'id; three of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>'s grandsons, Baysunghur, Ibrahim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> and Mohammad Juki, each commissioned a copy, and the Baysonghori Shahnameh of 1430 is preserved intact at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golestan-palace">Golestan Palace</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, inscribed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/memory-of-the-world-programme">Memory of the World Register</a> in 2007. Others fared worse: the Houghton Shahnameh and the Great Mongol Shahnameh were broken up in the 20th century so that their sheets could be sold separately, and a single sheet of the former was sold for £904,000 in 2006.</p><p>Printed editions began with the first volume of Mathew Lumsden's, published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Kolkata</a> in 1811. Turner Macan published the first complete edition in 1829 from 17 manuscripts, and Julius von Mohl another from 30 between 1838 and 1878, both based on late manuscripts. A Russian team led by E. E. Bertels published the first modern critical edition in Moscow in nine volumes (1960–1971), and Djalal Khaleghi-Motlagh, taking the Florence manuscript as his chief text, completed a new eight-volume critical edition in 2008.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/alexander-romance"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexander-romance.jpg" alt="Alexander Romance" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/alexander-romance">Alexander Romance</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alexander_romance._Armenian_illuminated_manuscript_of_XIV_century_(Venice,_San_Lazzaro,_424).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Some experts hold that Modern Persian has stayed more or less the language of Ferdowsi's time, over 1000 years ago, largely because of works like the Shahnameh, and later poets studied it to master the language. Later epics in Persian followed its style and method without reaching its fame. It introduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-romance">Alexander Romance</a> into Persian, turning Alexander from the negative figure of Zoroastrian writings into a positive one; the Iskandarnameh of Nizami and the Ayina-i Iskandari of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir-khusrau">Amir Khusrau</a> owe much to it.</p><p>Its stories spread beyond Persian. Names of its heroes appear in 11th- and 12th-century Georgian literature, indirect evidence of a lost Old Georgian translation, and the tales of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostam">Rostam</a> and Sohrab and of Bijan and Manijeh became part of Georgian folklore. English versions run from James Atkinson's of 1832 and the complete translation of Arthur and Edmond Warner (1905–1925) to the abridged ones of Dick Davis (2006) and Ahmad Sadri (2013); Dastur Faramroz Kutar and his brother put it into Gujarati, and Moniruddin Yusuf into Bengali.</p><p>Shahnameh-khani, the public recitation of the poem, has been practised for centuries in the cities and villages of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. Its stories became an opera, Kova the Blacksmith (1940), Indian films such as Rustom Sohrab (1963), a Tajikfilm trilogy and the animated film The Last Fiction (2017), and its millennial anniversary was marked in 2010 with exhibitions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a> and Washington.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Ferdowsi's Shahnameh, completed in 1010 after 33 years, put into Early New Persian verse the story of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>'s kings and heroes from the creation of the world to the Arab conquest, drawing on Sasanian chronicles and Persian prose. Copied, illustrated and recited for a thousand years, it shaped the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-language">Persian language</a> and the self-image of rulers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>.</em></p>

